now at least 41 people have been killed  in a fire at a prison in indonesia the  blaze at the tangarang jail near the  capital jakarta has now been put out  more than 78 people were injured well  jessica washington is at the scene in  tengarang and joins us live jessica so  just talk us through what more details  are now emerging about the fire  darn we're just outside the prison at  the main gate and we are still waiting  uh for information to come forward this  is still a developing situation as you  mentioned more than 70 people injured  among them we know that eight people  were critically injured and some of them  have burns to more than 98  of their body now so far the cause of  the fire is still being investigated  authorities say that potentially the  cause of the fire may have been an  electrical short circuit and that there  may have been a shortage of fire  extinguishers on the scene now local  authorities from the tangarang fire  department were able to extinguish the  blaze within about an hour but in that  time as you mentioned at least 41 people  lost their lives now it is important to  look at this safety incident in the  broader context we have known for many  years now that overcrowding has been a  problem in indonesian prisons and human  rights activists have long called to  rectify that issue and they've long  called for criminal justice reform uh in  particular to seek alternatives rather  than detention for those guilty of minor  offenses but despite those calls for  years  that has not yet happened and this  prison as we understand like many across  indonesia also faced overcrowding it had  capacity for around 600 prisoners but  there were more than 2 000 inmates at  the time of this fire now some prisons  in indonesia are at 300 to even 400  capacity and human rights activists say  that incidents such as what we have what  happened today simply should not happen  jessica thank you
 

